e born with natures. It's something I have reluctantly made peace with. People are born with natures. Everybody has the same human nature, but we are also born with our individual nature. The reason that I have struggled with accepting it is that in some ways it doesn't suggest pure free will. If you have a nature, It's you can overcome it. The bad parts anyway. I don't mean evil parts, just even bad parts. I mean, as I said often, I'm inherently lazy. So what I did is I've taken on so much work that I can't express my inherent laziness. So everybody has parts of their nature to fight. Everybody has some good parts to their nature. I'm not talking morally now. I'm talking productively or non-productively. or not but, and so I am saying that I have had this in my nature, a hatred of evil. I hated school bullies, and since I was always the biggest kid, I would often beat up the bully. My father was called to school so often that he just assumed any day he didn't get a call from the school was a good day. So I know that that's built in. But I also believe that the power of reason is great. And I could show people for whom hatred of evil is not built in. How they should in fact hate evil. It's why my favorite verse in the Bible is those of you who love God must hate evil. It's a command. People who has a command form in its verbs, English does not. So we add the word must. You must hate evil. If you don't hate evil, you don't love God. So I hate Hamas. I don't know why that's a toughy. Hezbollah, Hamas, the Iranian regime, the Nazis, the communist regimes. Really, I should do an hour at least on that subject. What stops people from hating evil? I haven't explored that at length. What is it in the human condition that pushes somebody to be neutral between Hamas and Israel? I'll play on both their houses. If there is a more obvious example of good versus evil in the world today, I'd like to know what it is. Right. Anyway, my Mizzouza campaign is catching on here. Uh, let'